  "doc.title": "How a CPU Does Math: Real Numbers, and Many at Once · A Hands-on Essay",
  "doc.desc": "A CPU does hundreds of billions of arithmetic operations on fractions every second, yet it cannot even store 0.1 exactly. This essay starts from 32 bits: how the bits of a floating-point number are laid out, ulps, rounding, subnormals, cancellation, FMA, the FP16, BF16, FP8 and block-scaled formats AI brought into hardware, and then SIMD, AVX-512, SVE and matrix units. A dozen hands-on models, every number computed bit by bit.",
