  "hero.title": "How a CPU Does Math: Real Numbers, and Many at Once",
  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Architecture · A hands-on essay",
  "hero.sub": "A CPU does hundreds of billions of floating-point operations a second, yet it cannot even store 0.1 exactly.<br>How does it store a fraction, how does it round, and how does one instruction compute a whole row?",
  "hero.lead": "The CPUs in phones and computers do billions to hundreds of billions of operations a second on numbers with a fractional part: the color of every pixel, every coordinate on a map, every layer of a neural network in a voice assistant. Yet not one of those numbers is exact; even 0.1 is not. This essay asks a single question: <strong>a CPU has only finitely many switches but must compute with infinitely many real numbers, so how does it do it, and what does it give up?</strong> The answer has two halves. The first half (chapters 1–8) is about one number: how its bits are laid out, how dense the grid is, what happens when a value falls between two grid points, how errors accumulate and get amplified, and why AI has cut the bit count all the way down to 8 and 4. The second half (chapters 9–12) is about a row of numbers: where the ability to compute 16, 64, or a whole block of a matrix with one instruction comes from, and what holds it back.",
  "hero.note": "The prerequisites are in <a href=\"../switches-that-compute/\">How a Pile of Switches Learned to Do Arithmetic</a> (how switches do addition, and why 0.1 + 0.2 is not 0.3) and <a href=\"../how-a-cpu-runs-a-program/\">How a CPU Runs a Program</a> (instructions and pipelines). Each model here is a scaled-down version of the real thing: floating-point numbers are rounded bit by bit with exact integer arithmetic, and SIMD and matrix units are counted at the register widths given in the manuals. This essay was written by an AI; the last paragraph of each chapter says what the model leaves out.",
  "ch1.title": "Fitting a Real Number into 32 Bits",
  "toc.ch1": "Fitting a Real Number into 32 Bits",
  "ch1.law": "A floating-point number is binary scientific notation: one sign bit, an exponent field that says where the binary point sits, and a mantissa field that records the significant digits. The number of bits is fixed, so only finitely many values can be stored, and every other number must be replaced by a nearby one.",
  "ch1.p1": "A calculator display with ten or so digits can show 6.02 × 10²³ and also 0.000000123. It splits a number into two parts: the significant digits (6.02) and where the decimal point goes (23 places). The first part sets \"how precise\", the second \"how big\". A floating-point number does the same thing, only in binary.",
  "ch1.p2": "The most common 32-bit format (float in C, called binary32 in the standard) splits its 32 bits into three fields: <strong>1 sign bit</strong>, <strong>8 exponent bits</strong>, and <strong>23 mantissa bits</strong>. The exponent field stores \"the true exponent plus 127\", so the 8-bit values 1 to 254 stand for −126 to +127; the two ends, 0 and 255, are reserved for special uses (chapter 4). That added 127 is called the <strong>bias</strong>. Any nonzero binary number has a leading 1, so that bit is not stored: the 23 mantissa bits are written after the binary point, with an implicit \"1.\" in front. This is the <strong>hidden bit</strong>, which lets 23 stored bits represent 24 significant bits. The value of a number is (−1)<sup>sign</sup> × 1.mantissa × 2<sup>exponent − 127</sup>.",
  "dg.f32.cap": "Figure 1: 0.1 stored as binary32, 32 bits. Red is the sign, cyan the exponent, gold the mantissa; the bits and values in the figure are computed by the page's model.",
  "dg.f32.aria": "Diagram: the 32 bits of binary32 split into a 1-bit sign, an 8-bit exponent and a 23-bit mantissa, filled in with 0.1",
  "dg.f32.ls": "Sign",
  "dg.f32.le": "Exponent (8 bits)",
  "dg.f32.lm": "Mantissa (23 bits)",
  "dg.f32.bs": "0",
