  "dg.f32.vm": "1.f = 1.6000000238418579",
  "dg.f32.hid": "hidden 1 before the point, not stored",
  "dg.f32.eq": "(+1) × 1.6000000… × 2⁻⁴ = 0.10000000149…",
  "dg.f32.eq1": "(+1) × 1.6000000… × 2⁻⁴",
  "dg.f32.eq2": "= 0.10000000149…",
  "ch1.p3": "Try it with 0.1. 0.1 = 1.6 × 2⁻⁴, so the exponent field is −4 + 127 = 123. In binary, 1.6 is 1.1001 1001 1001…, with the 1001 repeating forever (<a href=\"../switches-that-compute/#ch10\">Chapter 10 of How a Pile of Switches Learned to Do Arithmetic</a> explains why 0.1 cannot be written out exactly in binary). Twenty-three bits cannot hold an endless repeat, so the mantissa is rounded to nearest at bit 23, and the last bit rounds up: the full 32 bits are 0x3DCCCCCD. That is exactly 0.100000001490116119384765625, which is 1.490 × 10⁻⁹ larger than 0.1, a relative error of 1.49 × 10⁻⁸.",
  "ch1.model.title": "Store a number and see each of its bits",
  "ch1.model.how": "Enter a decimal (a fraction such as 1/3 also works), pick a format, and see which bits it is stored as, what is stored exactly, and how far off it is. Click any bit to flip it: flipping the lowest exponent bit doubles or halves the value; flipping the last mantissa bit moves it only slightly.",
